# Normalise to LF in the repository and on checkout, on every platform.
#
# Without this, Git on Windows checks out CRLF. Test fixtures then carry \r,
# generated files differ from what `render` writes, and the drift check fails
# for reasons that have nothing to do with the rules.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Fixtures encode exact bytes, including line endings, so they are never
# translated. A CRLF case in the corpus has to stay CRLF to be a CRLF case.
test/corpus/*.yml -text
*.snap -text

# Shell scripts must keep LF or they fail to execute.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Source is always diffable, whatever bytes end up in it.
#
# `src/safe-regex.ts` held a raw NUL inside a string literal, written as the
# byte rather than as the `\0` escape. Git classifies a file containing NUL as
# binary, so the regex screen, the one place where a mistake is a denial of
# service, had unreviewable diffs from the day it was written. Nobody noticed
# because a `Bin 0 -> 5694 bytes` line in a diffstat reads like a new asset.
*.ts text diff
*.js text diff
*.json text diff

# Binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
